PLACES=[
N('boardwalk-hall-atlantic-city','Boardwalk Hall (Atlantic City Convention Hall)','Atlantic City','njs','civic','standing',39.35478,-74.43843,'exact','open','drive','','1926–',
  'The city\'s great convention hall, designed by the firm Lockwood Greene and built from 1926 to 1929. It was the main convention centre until a new one opened in 1997, and it became a National Historic Landmark in 1987 as one of the few big buildings left from the resort\'s early years. It holds the world\'s largest musical instrument, a pipe organ of more than 33,000 pipes in eight chambers, two of them in the space between the roof and the ceiling. The Miss America pageant was held here for decades, and the Beatles played here on their first American tour in 1964.',
  'The long front on the Boardwalk and the vast arched hall inside. The organ was badly damaged at one time and has been under restoration; whether it can be heard or toured on a given day was not checked.',
  '','Still an arena and concert hall: the inside is seen by going to an event. Parking was not checked; the city has public garages near the Boardwalk.',
  [W('Boardwalk Hall'),['Boardwalk Hall: official page','https://www.boardwalkhall.com/']],conf=None,brick='?',hours='?'),
N('dennis-hotel-atlantic-city','Dennis Hotel (Dennis Tower, Bally\'s)','Atlantic City','njs','hotel','standing',39.35690,-74.43300,'site','open','drive','hotels','1860s–',
  'The Dennis began before the Civil War as a cottage on Michigan Avenue built by William Dennis, and grew into one of the big Boardwalk hotels: a French chateau-style addition in 1892, a six-storey east wing in 1906 and a west wing in 1910 by the Philadelphia architect Walter Smedley, and a seven-storey addition by Price and Walton in 1929. When Bally built its casino in 1978 and 1979 it tore down the neighbouring Marlborough-Blenheim but kept the Dennis, which is now the Dennis Tower of Bally\'s Atlantic City. In 2008 the row of shops in front of it was taken down to open the plaza again, and the tower\'s front was restored.',
  'The Dennis Tower faces the Boardwalk at Michigan Avenue, with its mansard roofs and dormers, behind the open plaza. Inside it is hotel rooms; original interiors were not checked.',
  '','A working casino hotel (Bally\'s Atlantic City). Look from the Boardwalk at any hour; parking in the hotel\'s own garage, fees not checked.',
  [W("Bally's Atlantic City")],conf=None,brick='?',hours='?'),
N('claridge-hotel-atlantic-city','The Claridge Hotel','Atlantic City','njs','hotel','standing',39.35773,-74.43192,'exact','open','drive','hotels','1930–',
  'A 24-storey tower hotel that opened in 1930, in the Depression, and was called "the Skyscraper by the Sea". It was the last of the great hotels built near the Boardwalk before the 1960s. Wikipedia names the Philadelphia builder John McShain as its designer; the hotel\'s own history calls him its builder, the man who went on to build the Pentagon and the Kennedy Center. It housed American forces in the Second World War, was a casino from 1981, became a tower of Bally\'s in 2002, and since 2014 has been a hotel again without gambling.',
  'The slim, setback tower stands back from the Boardwalk behind Brighton Park, between Park Place and Indiana Avenue. Interior not checked.',
  '','A working hotel. Its garage is on South Ohio Avenue, fees not checked; Brighton Park in front is open ground.',
  [W('The Claridge Hotel (Atlantic City)'),['The Claridge: history','https://www.claridge.com/history']],conf=None,brick='?',hours='?'),
N('absecon-lighthouse','Absecon Lighthouse','Atlantic City','njs','light','standing',39.36633,-74.41415,'exact','open','drive','','1854–',
  'New Jersey\'s tallest lighthouse, 171 feet, and the third-tallest masonry lighthouse in the country. It was designed by George Meade, begun in 1854 and first lit on January 15, 1857, and it was taken out of service in 1933, though a light still shows every night. It keeps its original first-order Fresnel lens. A copy of the 1925 keeper\'s house was built in 2002 and holds a museum and shop, and the old oil house shows a lens exhibit.',
  'You can climb the 228 steps to the watch room and the gallery outside. The original lens is in the lantern.',
  '','Run as a museum (abseconlighthouse.org). A donation is asked to climb, according to Wikipedia; the last climb is half an hour before closing. Mapped parking lots lie along Pacific Avenue nearby; whether they are free was not checked.',
  [W('Absecon Lighthouse'),['Absecon Lighthouse: hours','https://www.abseconlighthouse.org/']],conf=None,brick='?',
  hours='July and August daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; September to June Thursday to Monday 11 a.m. to 4 p.m. (closed Tuesday and Wednesday)'),
N('steel-pier-atlantic-city','Steel Pier','Atlantic City','njs','amuse','standing',39.35715,-74.41883,'site','open','walk','amusements','1897–',
  'An amusement pier of about 1,000 feet, built from 1897 by the Steel Pier Company and opened on June 18, 1898, on iron piles with a concrete understructure and steel girders. For the first seven decades of the 20th century it was one of the most popular places of entertainment in the country, with concerts, exhibits and rides, billed as "the Showplace of the Nation" and once 2,298 feet long. A storm carried off part of it in 1904 and it was rebuilt. Today it has some two dozen rides, an arcade and an observation wheel, and is owned by the Catanoso family.',
  'The pier runs out from the Boardwalk opposite the Hard Rock casino. What is left of the old pier\'s buildings was not checked.',
  '','Privately owned amusement pier, entered from the Boardwalk; ride tickets are sold. The hours on its page change with the season and the weather. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Steel Pier'),['Steel Pier: hours and contact','https://steelpier.com/contact/']],conf=None,brick='no',
  hours='Read late November: rides closed for the season; the Wheel Saturday noon to 8 p.m., Sunday noon to 7 p.m.'),
N('warner-theatre-atlantic-city','Warner Theatre','Atlantic City','njs','civic','standing',39.35528,-74.43389,'site','open','drive','','1929–',
  'A movie palace and stage theatre that Warner Bros. opened on the Boardwalk in June 1929, designed by Hoffman-Henon of Philadelphia, with a Spanish Moorish auditorium and a Venetian lobby. Bess Myerson was crowned Miss America here in 1945. Most of the theatre was torn down in the early 1960s, and what was left became a bowling alley, Boardwalk Bowl, in 1963. In 2023 Caesars Atlantic City and Spiegelworld made the site a theatre again, keeping the original front that faces the ocean.',
  'The 1929 ocean front on the Boardwalk. The inside is new work of 2023.',
  '','A working theatre and restaurant of Caesars Atlantic City. Times of shows not checked. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Warner Theatre (Atlantic City, New Jersey)')],conf=None,brick='?',hours='?'),
N('st-nicholas-tolentine-atlantic-city','St. Nicholas of Tolentine Church','Atlantic City','njs','worship','standing',39.36063,-74.42723,'exact','view','drive','','1905–',
  'A Roman Catholic church of rough-cut stone in the Romanesque Revival style, built in 1905 by John McShain to designs by the Philadelphia architect Edwin Forrest Durang, with a rear part added in 1935. Its stone rectory of 1908 and convent of 1927 stand beside it. It was listed on the National Register in 2001. It is one of the churches of the Parish of Saint Monica, and its 1916 Moller pipe organ was rebuilt in 2006.',
  'Twin towers, a triple main entrance and the original stained glass, all kept. Times when the church is open were not found.',
  '','A parish church of the Diocese of Camden (Parish of Saint Monica). Its Mass times could not be read from the parish\'s own page when this was written. Parking not checked.',
  [W('St. Nicholas of Tolentine Church'),INV],conf=None,brick='no'),
N('ww1-memorial-atlantic-city','World War I Memorial','Atlantic City','njs','curio','standing',39.35139,-74.45528,'site','view','drive','','1922–',
  'A round, columned memorial of 1922 in the Greek Revival manner, listed on the National Register in 1981. Inside the rotunda is a 9-foot bronze, "Liberty in Distress", by Frederick MacMonnies. Medallions for the Army, Navy, Marines and air service go round the frieze, and the battles in which the city\'s soldiers fought are cut into the stone above the columns.',
  'The statue stands inside the open rotunda.',
  '','A public memorial; who owns the ground round it was not checked, so look from the street. Mapped parking lies on North Albany Avenue next to it.',
  [W('World War I Memorial (Atlantic City, New Jersey)')],conf=None,brick='?'),
N('guarantee-trust-building-atlantic-city','Bartlett Building (Guarantee Trust Building)','Atlantic City','njs','civic','standing',39.36362,-74.42543,'site','view','drive','','1903–',
  'A seven-storey Beaux-Arts office block on Atlantic Avenue, built in 1903 for William H. Bartlett to designs by the Philadelphia architects Newman, Woodman and Harris. The Guarantee Trust Company had its banking floor on the ground floor, and the building took its name. It first had six storeys and a clock tower on the corner; about 1927 the tower came off and a seventh floor went on. Behind it on the same lot stand Bartlett\'s small brick power plant of 1903, which lit the block, and an older two-storey building of about 1882 that was moved here for the project.',
  'The stone entrance on Atlantic Avenue with its columns and broken pediment; the old cornice and window surrounds. The windows are replacements.',
  '','A commercial building; look from Atlantic Avenue. Parking not checked.',
  [INV],conf=None,brick='?'),
N('morris-guards-armory-atlantic-city','Morris Guards Armory','Atlantic City','njs','civic','standing',39.36083,-74.42918,'site','view','drive','','1901–',
  'The headquarters of the Morris Guards, a city drill and social club. The four-storey Tudor Revival front with crenellated corner towers was built in 1901 to designs by the Atlantic City architect Harold F. Adams, and a rear part followed in 1912 by the Philadelphia firm Davis and Davis. The club used it until 1987. It was partly rebuilt in 2023, and a plan to turn it into apartments promised to leave the front as it is.',
  '"ARMORY MORRIS GUARDS" on the battlemented parapet, the stone oriel window, and leaded glass in the arched first-floor windows.',
  '','Private; look from South New York Avenue. Parking not checked.',
  [INV,['Jersey Digs: Adaptive reuse plan for the Morris Guard Armory','https://jerseydigs.com/adaptive-reuse-plan-for-atlantic-citys-historic-morris-guard-armory-advances/']],conf=None,brick='?'),
N('knights-of-columbus-hotel-atlantic-city','Knights of Columbus Club-Hotel','Atlantic City','njs','hotel','standing',39.36007,-74.42731,'site','view','drive','','1927–',
  'A five-storey Spanish Revival club and hotel built in 1927 for the Knights of Columbus, designed by Frank A. Berry. It is richly trimmed with glazed tile, carved stone, iron balconies and decorated door surrounds. It stands empty, with its doors boarded, and the city named it a landmark in 2024.',
  'The tiled and carved doorways and the iron balconies on Pacific Avenue.',
  '','Empty and closed; look from Pacific Avenue. Parking not checked.',
  [INV],conf=None,brick='?'),
N('madison-hotel-atlantic-city','Madison Hotel','Atlantic City','njs','hotel','standing',39.35841,-74.42998,'site','view','drive','hotels','1929–',
  'A 14-storey hotel designed by Victor Gondos Jr. of Philadelphia and opened in 1929, just as the Depression began; it was listed on the National Register in 1984. After a bankruptcy in the 1960s it became a wing of the Sands casino next door, and after the Sands closed in 2006 it was sold and reopened in 2014 as a chain hotel. It had closed again by July 2023; the city cleared people sheltering in it with no water or power and boarded the ground floor. It was offered for sale as it stands in November 2024.',
  'The tall hotel front on Martin Luther King Boulevard. Boarded and empty.',
  '','Private and closed; look from the street. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Madison Hotel (Atlantic City)')],conf=None,brick='?'),
N('central-methodist-church-atlantic-city','Central Methodist Episcopal Church (Asbury United Methodist)','Atlantic City','njs','worship','standing',39.36156,-74.42549,'site','view','drive','','1895–',
  'A brick Gothic Revival church of 1923 on Pacific Avenue, with a stone-and-frame part of 1895 behind it that now holds the chancel. The congregation in it now is Asbury United Methodist Church.',
  'Stone window surrounds and entry arches, and a large pointed stained-glass window over the paired wooden doors. Interior not checked.',
  '','A working church; service times were not checked. Look from Pacific Avenue. Parking not checked.',
  [INV],conf=None,brick='?'),
]
